    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d554">

                <pb facs="0493" n="483" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg483"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.001 Chiang Kai-shek/8–1149: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Taipei</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Macdonald</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Taipei</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-08-11T15:00:00+09:00">August 11, 1949—3
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 12—3:57 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>375. Visited General Sun Li-jen<anchor n="75" xml:id="d554-f1"/> at his
                    headquarters during official visit South Taiwan. He looked tired, despondent and
                    holds little hope <pb facs="0494" n="484" xml:id="pg484"/> for Nationalist
                    forces. He severely criticized Government, saying officials only interested
                    security their positions. Numerous conferences have accomplished nothing,
                    according him. He said Government is disintegrating due its own corruption and
                    rottenness and Gimo is badly mistaken if he thinks he can hold it together
                    through personal loyalty as leader. Sun claims Gimo made great mistake by not
                    retiring entirely from political scene and any hope of Acting President and Gimo
                    reaching complete agreement or solidarity and working together on major issues
                    is pure figment of imagination.</p>
                <p>He foresees total collapse of Nationalist armies, saying armed forces no longer
                    have will to resist and that civilians have lost all confidence in President,
                    Government. He remarked that it is so hopeless he can’t see why they carry on.
                    Sun impressed me as having taken just about as much as he can stand and about
                    ready to give up but continues to do his duty out of sheer patriotism.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 375, repeated Canton 176.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Macdonald</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d554-f1" n="75" xml:id="d554-f1-content">
                <p>Commanding General Taiwan Defense Headquarters and Deputy Director of Military
                    and Political Affairs for Southeast China.</p>
            </note>
